raw_response:
 To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function. This function takes one argument, which is an integer value. The value can be any non-negative integer, and it specifies the exit status code that is returned to the operating system.

Here's an example of how to use `exit()` to terminate a C program:
```
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    // Do some work here...

    // When you're done, use exit() to terminate the program
    exit(0);
}
```
When the `exit()` function is called, the program will exit immediately, and the operating system will return the specified exit status code to any parent processes that were running the program.

If you don't specify an argument for `exit()`, the function will use a default value of 0, which indicates that the program terminated normally. If you specify a non-zero value for the argument, the function will use that value as the exit status code, which can be used by parent processes to determine how the program terminated.

It's important to note that using `exit()` to terminate a program is generally considered good practice, because it allows the operating system to clean up any resources that the program was using, and it ensures that any open files or network connections are properly closed. If you don't use `exit()` to terminate a program, the operating system may not be able to clean up these resources properly, which can cause problems.

